# Sorting Algorithms: Ordering Data Efficiently

> Sorting — putting a collection into order — is the most studied
> problem in computer science. This lesson walks the simple quadratic
> sorts, the fast divide-and-conquer family, and how to choose.

## 1. Why Sorting Matters

Sorted data is dramatically cheaper to work with:

- **Binary search** needs sorted input: O(log n) instead of O(n).
- **Duplicates** collapse into adjacent runs, so dedup is one scan.
- People expect ordered output: price lists, rankings, inbox dates.

## 2. Vocabulary: In-Place and Stable

[definition]
A sort is **in-place** if it rearranges elements inside the original
array using only O(1) extra memory. A sort is **stable** if equal keys
keep their original relative order, so a later sort never scrambles
an earlier one.
[/definition]

## 3. The Simple Sorts: Bubble, Selection, Insertion

| Algorithm | How it works | Best | Worst |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Bubble | Swap adjacent out-of-order pairs; the largest value bubbles to the end each pass | O(n) | O(n²) |
| Selection | Scan for the smallest remaining item, swap it into place | O(n²) | O(n²) |
| Insertion | Slide each new item left into its spot, like arranging cards in hand | O(n) | O(n²) |

All three use O(1) extra space. Insertion sort is the keeper — adaptive,
finishing nearly-sorted input in O(n) — and the pick for short runs.

## 4. Bubble Sort, Pass by Pass

Sorting the list 5 3 8 1 4 with left-to-right passes:

| Pass | Swaps | List after the pass |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | 3 | 3 5 1 4 8 |
| 2 | 2 | 3 1 4 5 8 |
| 3 | 1 | 1 3 4 5 8 |
| 4 | 0 | 1 3 4 5 8 — sorted, stop early |

```text
BUBBLE-SORT(A):
  repeat until a full pass makes no swap:
    for i in 0 .. length(A) - 2:
      if A[i] > A[i+1]:
        swap A[i] and A[i+1]
```

[tip]
The "no swaps means done" rule gives bubble sort its O(n) best case.
[/tip]

## 5. Efficient Sorts: Divide and Conquer

**Merge sort** splits the list in half, recursively sorts each half,
then merges the two sorted halves in one linear sweep:

@diagram tree id="sorting-split-tree" layout="tree" aria="Merge sort splitting a list into halves down to single elements"
  "38 27 43 3" -> "38 27"
  "38 27 43 3" -> "43 3"
  "38 27" -> "38"
  "38 27" -> "27"
  "43 3" -> "43"
  "43 3" -> "3"
@/diagram

The tree has log₂ n levels, each costing O(n) to merge, so merge sort
runs in O(n log n) on every input — but needs the O(n) scratch buffer.

**Quicksort** picks a **pivot**, partitions smaller items left and
larger right, then recurses on both sides: in-place and usually
fastest, but a bad pivot on sorted input degrades it to O(n²).

**Heapsort** builds a max-heap, then repeatedly swaps the root (the
maximum) to the end and re-heapifies: O(n log n) guaranteed, in-place,
but not stable and slower than quicksort on most hardware.

## 6. Beyond Comparisons: Counting and Bucket Sort

Every comparison-based sort needs Ω(n log n) comparisons in the worst
case: n! orderings exist, and each comparison cuts them in half.
Non-comparison sorts dodge the bound by using keys as array indices:

| Algorithm | Idea | Time | Works when |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Counting | Tally how many times each key occurs, rebuild in order | O(n + k) | small integer keys, k = range |
| Bucket | Scatter items into ordered buckets, sort each bucket | O(n + k) avg | data spread evenly over a range |

[important]
Counting sort breaks the n log n barrier by not comparing at all — it
indexes: a million exam scores between 0 and 100 sort in linear time.
[/important]

## 7. The Master Comparison Table

| Algorithm | Best | Average | Worst | Extra space | Stable |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Bubble | O(n) | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(1) | Yes |
| Selection | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(1) | No |
| Insertion | O(n) | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(1) | Yes |
| Merge | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n) | Yes |
| Quick | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n²) | O(log n) | No |
| Heap | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(1) | No |
| Counting | O(n + k) | O(n + k) | O(n + k) | O(k) | Yes |

## 8. Choosing a Sort in Practice

@chart bar title="Approximate comparisons to sort 1,000 items"
labels:
Bubble sort
Insertion sort
Merge sort
Quicksort

data:
495000
250000
10000
11000
@/chart

In practice, reach for the built-in sort and move on — Python and Java
ship **Timsort**, a stable merge/insertion hybrid that runs in O(n) on
nearly-sorted data; C++ `std::sort` uses **introsort**, quicksort that
turns to heapsort when recursion runs deep.

- Guaranteed stability? Timsort. Tiny arrays? Insertion sort.
  Small integer keys? Counting sort.

[summary]
The simple sorts are O(n²) and only suit tiny or nearly-sorted inputs.
Merge, quick, and heap all average O(n log n): merge stable but O(n)
space; quick in-place with an O(n²) worst case; heap safe but unstable.
Counting sort reaches O(n + k) by abandoning comparisons.
[/summary]
